 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Optimization Balance:** The learning rates for the latent codes ($\mathbf{z}$) and the network weights ($\theta$) must be balanced. If $\mathbf{z}$ learns too fast, the network may fail to generalize; if $\theta$ learns too fast, the latent codes may not capture the necessary details [S1].
-*   **Overfitting:** Since $\mathbf{z}$ is optimized per image, the method can overfit to the training set if not regularized. This is distinct from generative models that learn a distribution; GLO memorizes the training data via the latent codes [S1].
+*   **Optimization Stability:** Since both parameters and latents are optimized, the problem is non-convex. Careful learning rate scheduling is required to prevent divergence [S1].
+*   **Auxiliary Inputs:** In NeRF-based applications, $u_i$ (such as position and direction) must be provided correctly for each sample [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [GAN Inversion Encoder and Optimization](16692-gan-inversion-encoder-and-optimization.md)
-Extends GLO by using a learned encoder to initialize the latent codes, refining them via optimization to reconstruct the scene. It operates on a structured latent grid conditioned on camera poses, allowing for spatially varying scene representations and editing.
+Enables view synthesis and scene completion by inverting a trained generative radiance field into a latent code grid. It uses a local coordinate system robust to rearrangements, unlike global coordinate methods.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is significantly higher than standard supervised learning because the optimization loop must update both the network weights and the latent codes for every image. The number of parameters scales with $N \times d$ (number of images $\times$ latent dimension) plus the network size.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing $N$ latent codes in memory. For large datasets, this can be substantial.
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; does not require a discriminator; effectively optimizes the scene representation for a specific dataset.
-*   **Limitations:** Not inherently generative (does not learn a distribution over scenes); prone to overfitting if not regularized; requires joint optimization of latent codes and weights, which can be unstable [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When the goal is to optimize a scene representation for a fixed set of images (e.g., NeRF-W, Edit-NeRF) and the latent codes are used to account for appearance changes or transient effects [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** Time complexity is proportional to the number of samples and iterations. Memory complexity includes storing the latent codes for all samples.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement as it avoids the complexity of adversarial training. Effective for fitting specific scenes or views.
+*   **Limitations:** May overfit to the training data if not regularized. Does not provide a generative prior for unseen data in the same way a full GAN does.
+*   **When to use:** When the goal is to reconstruct or condition on a specific set of images rather than generating novel, diverse samples from a learned distribution.
 
 ## Background
-Generative Latent Optimization was introduced by Karras et al. in 2017 [S1]. It was originally proposed as a method for training generative models without a discriminator. In the context of 3D vision and NeRF, it has been adopted to optimize latent codes that condition the radiance field, allowing for the representation of appearance variations and transient objects in unstructured image collections (e.g., NeRF-W) [S1].
+GLO was introduced as a method to train generative models without discriminators [S1]. It has been widely adopted in the 2020–2023 era for NeRF models that use latent codes to account for changeable aspects of scenes, such as appearance or transient effects (e.g., NeRF-W) [S1]. It is distinct from methods that use latent codes primarily for scene editing, although it shares the underlying optimization mechanism.
 
